# Taxonomic Name Italics

Per zoological nomenclature convention, only **genus** and **species** (and subspecies)
names are italicized. Higher taxa — order, family, superfamily, class, etc. — are
written in normal (roman) text.

- Italicize: genus alone (`Centruroides`), genus + species (`Centruroides sculpturatus`),
  or genus abbreviated with species (`C. sculpturatus`).
- Do NOT italicize: order (`Scorpiones`), family (`Buthidae`, `Scorpionidae`),
  superfamily, or other ranks above genus.
- Author citations following a genus/species name (e.g. `Peters, 1861`) are not
  italicized, even when adjacent to an italicized name.

```text
✅ the genus *Centruroides* (Buthidae)
✅ *Hemiscorpius* Peters, 1861 (Hemiscorpiidae Pocock, 1893)
✅ the order Scorpiones includes the family Scorpionidae
❌ the genus *Centruroides* (*Buthidae*)
❌ the order *Scorpiones* includes the family *Scorpionidae*
```

## In this codebase

- JSON content (`content/**/*.json`, e.g. `people/*.json`, `publications.json`)
  wraps italic spans in `*asterisks*`, rendered via `InlineEmphasis`/`MarkdownContent`.
- Inline JSX prose (e.g. `app/**/sections/*.tsx`, `app/arachnids/ArachnidsClient.tsx`)
  wraps italic spans in `<em>...</em>`.
- Bibliographic `citationHtml` fields use `<i>...</i>`.

In all three cases, apply the same rule: wrap only the genus/species portion, leave
order/family/superfamily names outside the emphasis markup.

## Precedence over incoming content

This rule overrides any italics styling present in incoming content — copy pasted
from authors, migrated from legacy pages, imported from bibliographic databases
(e.g. Crossref/JATS XML), or otherwise supplied pre-formatted. If incoming text
italicizes a higher taxon (or fails to italicize a genus/species name), correct the
markup to match this convention rather than preserving the source formatting as-is.
